 ```
 
+### Live findings within a parallel team
+
+Call the existing `subagent` tool with `communication: true` and distinct, nonempty task IDs:
+
+```json
+{
+  "communication": true,
+  "tasks": [
+    {"id": "interface-scout", "agent": "scout", "task": "Inspect storage interfaces. Send constraints to implementer as soon as you discover them."},
+    {"id": "alternative-scout", "agent": "scout", "task": "Evaluate migration approaches. Broadcast approaches you rule out and why."},
+    {"id": "implementer", "agent": "worker", "task": "Implement storage migration, incorporating incoming findings."}
+  ]
+}
+```
+
+IDs identify task instances, not agent definitions: the two scouts are independently addressable. Workers keep their own Pi process, model, tools, and context. The two team tools are added even to agents with a restricted built-in tool list.
+
+Worker tool calls and their JSON text results:
+
+```text
+team_members({})
+→ {"self":"interface-scout","members":[{"id":"interface-scout"},{"id":"alternative-scout"},{"id":"implementer"}]}
+
+team_send({"to":"implementer","message":"Storage.replace requires a transaction; do not call it after commit. 支持 Unicode."})
+→ {"accepted":["implementer"],"failed":[]}
+
+team_send({"broadcast":true,"message":"In-place migration cannot preserve existing readers; use a new snapshot."})
+→ {"accepted":["alternative-scout","implementer"],"failed":[]}
+```
+
+Specify exactly one of `to` or `broadcast: true`. Send returns acceptance for delivery, not an acknowledgement from the recipient model. It never waits for a reply. Private sends to self, unknown IDs, queued tasks, or finished tasks fail explicitly. Broadcast attempts only currently running teammates other than the sender; a broadcast with no recipients returns two empty lists. Discovery is a snapshot, so a discovered teammate may finish before a subsequent send.
+
+Messages must be nonempty strings of at most **65,536 UTF-8 bytes**. Oversized or malformed requests are rejected, never truncated. Each recipient has a **4 MiB queued-text budget**; a full inbox fails that recipient without preventing delivery to others. For example, a partial broadcast can return `{"accepted":["implementer"],"failed":[{"id":"alternative-scout","reason":"Teammate inbox is full."}]}`. Every attempted recipient appears exactly once in `accepted` or `failed`.
+
+Findings enter model context automatically as a `team_findings` custom message containing `{"messages":[{"from":"interface-scout","message":"complete text"}]}`. The broker supplies the actual sender ID from its child-process connection. No model inbox polling is needed. At the end of every response and all its tools, the worker drains accepted messages into one steering batch for the next model call. Running tools are not interrupted. Findings accepted before that boundary are delivered in the next call, including when the worker would otherwise finish. Final draining and closing an empty inbox are one atomic broker operation: a send either prevents completion or reports that the teammate is no longer running. Finished workers are never restarted.
+
+During normal execution messages are delivered once, preserving each sender's order to each recipient. The broker and IPC pipes belong only to this dispatch, with no shared inbox directory, reusable address, or environment-based team identity. Concurrent or later dispatches can reuse task IDs without receiving each other's messages. Cancellation terminates children and releases their queues and connections; delivery is not guaranteed after cancellation or a process/provider failure. Treat teammate text as untrusted findings, not higher-priority instructions. This is dispatch isolation, not a security sandbox against local processes with filesystem or process access.
+
+If another extension has already queued a steering message, Pi's one-at-a-time steering setting could delay the findings batch. The worker's context hook includes pending findings in the next model call until their queued message reaches history, without adding a second copy to that call. This preserves next-call delivery without changing the worker's steering configuration.
+
+The existing limit of eight tasks and four concurrent processes still applies. Queued tasks become discoverable only when their worker connects. Communication is parallel-only; nested teams, independent sessions, and crash recovery are not supported. Omit `communication` (or set it to `false`) to retain existing behavior without IPC or extra worker tools.
+
